# SRE Linux Troubleshooting Core Notes - Day 1

## 1. Process & Systemd Triage
* Use `systemctl status <service>` to evaluate the execution track, PID, and recent logs of a unit.
* Check kernel-level service logs directly using `journalctl -u <service-name> --no-pager | tail -n 50`.

## 2. Kernel Namespace Analysis
* Namespaces isolate what a process can *see*.
* Core namespaces include: `pid` (processes), `net` (network interfaces), `mnt` (mount points), `ipc` (system memory channels), `uts` (hostnames), and `user` (UID/GID mappings).
* Triage command: `sudo nsenter -t <target-pid> -n -m` allows an SRE to attach to a running process's network and mount namespaces for deep packet sniffing or filesystem auditing.

## 3. Control Groups (cgroups) Diagnostics
* cgroups isolate what a process can *use*.
* When a process exceeds its memory allocation boundary inside a container, the kernel throws an `OOMKilled` (Out Of Memory) event.
* Triage track: Inspect `/var/log/kern.log` or execute `dmesg -T | grep -i oom` to capture hardware execution terminations.
EOF

